Subject: Give a dependent qualified name a type instead of an opaque blob

A member whose type is spelled through a template parameter:

    struct InnerType {
        typedef int related_type;
        int foo;
    };

    template <typename ContainedType> class Container {
        typename ContainedType::related_type contents_;
    };

    typedef Container<InnerType> Concrete;

has no type until `ContainedType` is known, and there is nothing in the
IR which can stand for "the `related_type` of whatever this is
instantiated with". So `contents_` becomes an opaque type of no known
size, `ContainedType` is then used by nothing and is dropped as unused,
and `Container` - along with every type holding one, `Concrete`
included - is left as a blob. That is what
https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-bindgen/issues/1924 reports.

Rust can say it, though, through an associated type. This adds a
`TypeKind::DependentQualifiedType`, which remembers the type parameter
it qualifies and the name it looks up, and renders it as a trait
implemented by each type which has such an inner type:

    pub struct InnerType {
        pub foo: ::std::os::raw::c_int,
    }
    pub type InnerType_related_type = ::std::os::raw::c_int;
    pub trait __bindgen_has_inner_type_related_type {
        type related_type: std::fmt::Debug + Default + Copy + Clone;
    }
    impl __bindgen_has_inner_type_related_type for InnerType {
        type related_type = InnerType_related_type;
    }
    pub struct Container<ContainedType>
    where
        ContainedType: __bindgen_has_inner_type_related_type,
    {
        pub contents_:
            <ContainedType as __bindgen_has_inner_type_related_type>::related_type,
        pub _phantom_0:
            ::std::marker::PhantomData<::std::cell::UnsafeCell<ContainedType>>,
    }
    pub type Concrete = Container<InnerType>;

which is the type C++ has, and `Concrete` now has a layout rather than a
blob where its contents should be.

It is off by default, behind `--dependent-qualified-types`, because the
trait is a bound and not every instantiation can satisfy it: a type
bindgen cannot see inside - blocklisted, opaque, or `replaces=`d by a
substitute - has no inner types to implement the trait with, so
`Container<ThatType>` does not compile where before it was a blob that
did. Where every type a template is instantiated with is one bindgen
generates in full, which is the case this exists for, the option is
what turns the blob into the type.

Recognising the member is `clang.rs`'s job and it already did most of
it - but had stopped doing it. The spelling it matches on is what clang
prints, and clang stopped printing the `typename` keyword in it: as of
clang 21 the canonical spelling of `typename T::Inner` is
`type-parameter-0-0::Inner`, so a pattern insisting on the keyword matches
nothing and such a member is taken for the parameter itself - `contents_:
T` rather than an opaque type of its own. The keyword is now optional,
which is a fix on its own account, and `get_associated_type_name` joins
`is_associated_type` to keep the name - where the name qualified is a single identifier, which is
what an inner type of the parameter can be. `T::Inner::Deeper` is not
one, and stays the opaque type it was.

A dependent qualified name is a second thing which can hang off one
template-parameter cursor, so the context's cursor-to-parameter map gains
an inner map keyed by the name qualified - `None` for the parameter
itself. What stops the parameter being dropped as unused is the
used-template-parameter analysis learning that a dependent qualified type
uses both itself and the parameter it qualifies, reached through the new
`DependentQualifiedTypeParam` edge.

A member names such a type through any number of pointers, references,
arrays and function signatures - `const typename STRING_TYPE::value_type*
ptr_` is the shape this was written for - so the walk which looks for them
follows those, stopping at another comp, whose parameters are its own
business. It carries the set of types already walked, because a template
argument can name the instantiation it is an argument of. Each comp
records what that walk found, which is what its
`ContainedDependentQualifiedType` edges are traced from, and it runs
before the graph is frozen so that those edges are there to be followed.

A trait is emitted only for a name some dependent qualified type being
generated actually looks up, so a header full of inner typedefs nobody
reaches through does not acquire a trait apiece - and a comp which is not
generated does not leave behind an impl of a trait that went with it.
Walking a comp's members resolves through type refs and aliases while that
comp is on loan from the context, hence `ItemResolver::resolve_fallible`,
which answers `None` rather than panicking for the item that is out.

A class names such a type in either of two places, and both are collected,
apart. A field of the projected type is one, and then the class itself needs
the bound: its own layout depends on the projection. An inner type of the
class is the other - `using period = typename Period::type;` - and there the
class may hold nothing of that type at all, so the bound belongs on the impl
saying the class has that inner type and not on the class.

Putting it on the class instead is what `std::chrono::duration` shows to be
wrong. It holds only its `rep`, and declares `period` through its other
parameter; bounding the class for the sake of that typedef stops it being
instantiated over a period whose own type is one bindgen cannot see inside,
which is what a standard library which hides `ratio` behind an
implementation-detail namespace hands it. Bounding the impl instead leaves the
class open and still lets `time_point`, which projects `rep` and `period` off
the duration it is given, find what it needs.

Collecting neither left a class whose parameter only an inner type names
rendered without that parameter, while the alias for the inner type and the
impl were both written in terms of it: three spellings of one class which
disagreed, two of them naming something not in scope. Both kinds are traced
from the class, so the parameter counts as used either way and the class is
rendered with it.

Three things keep a bound from being asked for where it is not wanted. An
opaque class is skipped: it is rendered as a blob of the right size with no
fields at all, and a used parameter would give it the phantom field which
stands for one, which such a class is asserted not to have. An opaque inner
type is not walked through: the name it looks up is not generated with it, so
a bound would only narrow what the class can be instantiated over. And a bound
is put on a parameter only where the trait it names is declared, which is for
the names of the dependent qualified types which are generated, so that
nothing is left naming a trait nobody declared. Opacity is worked out before
any class is on loan from the context, because `Item::is_opaque` resolves an
item's ancestors and one of those is the class being recorded.

An impl is emitted only where it can hold. Not where its associated type
names a parameter the class was not rendered with - `template <class C, class
T> struct iterator { typedef T value_type; };` has no fields at all, so `T` is
used by nothing and an impl assigning `iterator_value_type<T>` would name a
`T` not in scope; saying nothing there leaves the class not implementing the
trait, where it stood before this option existed. And what the trait asks of
its associated type the impl asks of the type it assigns, as a bound of its
own: `typedef R rep;` assigns a bare parameter, which satisfies none of it, so
without that the impl compiled for no instantiation at all, and with it the
class implements the trait for the arguments which do satisfy it.

The trait is declared in the root module, because that is where the
dependent qualified types which need it are parented, so every reference
to one names it through that module: under `--enable-cxx-namespaces` a
bound written bare resolves in the root module and nowhere else.

Three shapes this does not cover, each of which stops the bindings
compiling rather than falling back to the blob, which is the rest of why
it is off by default.

The associated type's bounds are what the dependent qualified type itself
derives, on the assumption that every inner type reached through the trait
derives the same set; an inner type which derives less does not satisfy
them.

A class holding an instantiation which needs the bound - `Outer<T>` holding
a `Container<T>` - does not acquire it, and neither does one which has such
a class as a base: the walk stops at another comp, whose parameters are its
own business, so what that comp requires of them is not read back out. The
parameter is used, and the class is rendered with it; only the bound is
missing, so the bindings do not compile.

Reading it out means inferring a requirement which nothing in this class
spells, by mapping an instantiation's arguments onto its definition's
parameters and substituting through them, to a fixed point, since what holds
one may itself be held. It does not need the associated-type bounds moved
off the trait, and it does not need a projection in the IR to point at - a
table of parameter and inner name apiece would carry it. What it does need
is an answer for an argument which is not simply a parameter, `Base<Wrap<T>>`
wanting a predicate on the substituted type rather than on a parameter, and
for parameter packs and partial specializations.

A class implements the trait for each inner type it declares, and an
inherited one is not among them, so a template instantiated over a derived
class whose base holds the inner type has no impl to reach.
